      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does thermoformed mean in pickleball paddles?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It means the face and edge are heat-formed and pressed together around the core into a single sealed shell, often called unibody construction. Many thermoformed paddles also inject foam into the perimeter. The result is a stiffer, more stable and usually more powerful paddle than traditional assembled designs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are thermoformed paddles more powerful?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally yes. The sealed, stiffer structure returns more energy to the ball, and players and brands commonly describe thermoformed paddles as noticeably more powerful than traditional paddles of the same thickness. Rule changes have since aimed to limit extreme rebound power in approved paddles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do thermoformed paddles break in?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many owners report that some thermoformed paddles become livelier after a period of play. Reports vary by model, and manufacturers have revised designs. If you are concerned, read recent feedback on the exact paddle and choose one from a brand with a clear warranty."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are thermoformed paddles legal in tournaments?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many are, but only specific approved models and versions. USA Pickleball has tightened testing and removed some paddles from its approved list. Check the current official list for the exact model before using it in sanctioned play, and check other organizations' lists if you play their events."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are thermoformed paddles good for beginners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not as a first paddle. The extra power can make soft shots harder to control. Beginners tend to learn faster with a forgiving traditional 16mm paddle. Once dinks and resets are consistent, a thermoformed paddle can add useful power and stability."
