          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying thermoformed as a beginner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The extra power can make dinks and resets harder to keep low, which slows learning. Newer players usually progress faster with a forgiving traditional 16mm paddle, then upgrade once their touch is reliable and they know they want more pace.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring approval status</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thermoformed paddles have faced the most rule changes. If you play sanctioned events, check that the exact model and version is on the current approved list. A paddle that was approved earlier, or a similar-looking version, may not be.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every thermoformed paddle is the same</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Construction details vary: foam placement, core thickness, face layup and weight. Two thermoformed paddles can feel very different. Compare specific models, read recent owner reports and demo if possible, rather than buying on the label alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the warranty check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Owners have reported core and face issues on some paddles. Without reliable failure data, the warranty is your best protection. Choose a brand that clearly covers core crush and delamination, and register the paddle if required.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to fix control problems</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thermoforming adds power and stability, not touch. If your dinks float and resets pop up, a hotter paddle will usually make that worse. Address technique first, or choose a softer traditional paddle until your soft game is consistent.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does thermoformed mean in pickleball paddles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means the face and edge are heat-formed and pressed together around the core into a single sealed shell, often called unibody construction. Many thermoformed paddles also inject foam into the perimeter. The result is a stiffer, more stable and usually more powerful paddle than traditional assembled designs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are thermoformed paddles more powerful?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes. The sealed, stiffer structure returns more energy to the ball, and players and brands commonly describe thermoformed paddles as noticeably more powerful than traditional paddles of the same thickness. Rule changes have since aimed to limit extreme rebound power in approved paddles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do thermoformed paddles break in?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many owners report that some thermoformed paddles become livelier after a period of play. Reports vary by model, and manufacturers have revised designs. If you are concerned, read recent feedback on the exact paddle and choose one from a brand with a clear warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are thermoformed paddles legal in tournaments?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many are, but only specific approved models and versions. USA Pickleball has tightened testing and removed some paddles from its approved list. Check the current official list for the exact model before using it in sanctioned play, and check other organizations' lists if you play their events.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are thermoformed paddles good for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not as a first paddle. The extra power can make soft shots harder to control. Beginners tend to learn faster with a forgiving traditional 16mm paddle. Once dinks and resets are consistent, a thermoformed paddle can add useful power and stability.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do thermoformed paddles last as long?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no reliable public data comparing lifespans. Owners have reported core crush and delamination on some paddles of all types. Choose a well-reviewed model with a strong warranty, store it away from heat, and watch for dead spots or changes in sound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a Gen 3 or Gen 4 paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                These are informal marketing labels. Gen 3 usually refers to the thermoformed, foam-edged raw carbon paddles that became popular around 2023. Gen 4 often refers to later designs with fuller foam cores or other changes. Compare actual construction details rather than relying on generation names.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

